WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.920
I build a lot of decks, or at least, I start&nbsp;
working on a lot of decks. However, the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.560
majority of these die before ever reaching&nbsp;
completion and acquisition. Most of the time I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.960
won’t get very far at all—I’ll spend maybe 20 or&nbsp;
30 minutes digging around and throwing stuff in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.520
a list before identifying something I don’t like&nbsp;
about the deck, usually either that I don’t like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.400
the plan I had in mind, or that the plan I had in&nbsp;
mind isn’t going to be consistently executable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.440
In this video I want to talk about a different set&nbsp;
of decks, though—the decks that pass that initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.160
weed-out phase, that seem like promising ideas,&nbsp;
that I spend hours completing a somewhat polished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.760
list for, but which I have also since abandoned,&nbsp;
never to be returned to. To me these are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.160
drafts clogging up my Archidekt account that I&nbsp;
might look back at as a reference point later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.160
but I think they can actually serve as an&nbsp;
instructive tool for thinking about what makes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:55.120
deck idea that initially seems good actually good,&nbsp;
truly worth constructing and trying to play with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.240
Because the reality is that a lot of decks don’t&nbsp;
actually deserve to be finished, and being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.160
identify when you shouldn’t finish a deck based&nbsp;
on your own preferences will save you precious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.640
time and prevent you from boring yourself&nbsp;
trying to play dead-on-arrival junk piles.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.240
I want to begin with perhaps the most obviously&nbsp;
flawed idea—a Rograkh/Thrasios polymorph deck I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.840
nicknamed “turbostax”. The idea here was pretty&nbsp;
simple—Rograkh is a zero-cost creature in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.640
command zone, which gives an easy Polymorph&nbsp;
target, and Thrasios is a sweet utility creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.040
that gets me into two other colors I want to be&nbsp;
in. The eight creatures we’re using our Polymorph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.440
spells to spin into are mostly big, generic bombs&nbsp;
with some sort of nasty way to slow down our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.840
opponents attached—Niv-Mizzet Parun, Hullbreaker&nbsp;
Horror, two different Jin-Gitaxiai—the works. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.800
top of this, 3 of our 10 creature Polymorph&nbsp;
spells also target artifacts, and we add an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.160
extra artifact Polymorph spell to help us pop out&nbsp;
one of three key artifacts: Nexus of Becoming as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.280
an engine, God-Pharaoh’s Statue as a powerful stax&nbsp;
piece, and Proteus Staff as a repeatable polymorph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.240
effect. To support this dual-type-polymorphing&nbsp;
gameplan, the deck would run a bunch of creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.800
token generators and even more artifact creature&nbsp;
token generators, which were mostly built into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.120
the deck’s supporting categories like ramp&nbsp;
and removal. The overall goal was for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.000
deck to be a flexible midrange deck, able to do&nbsp;
cheesy polymorph plays but also able to pivot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.720
into grindier gameplans and crunch value into the&nbsp;
late game with Thrasios. And, that flexibility was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.760
precisely what I found appealing about the deck.&nbsp;
Like other cheese strategies such as reanimator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.720
Polymorph can feel a little all-or-nothing: either&nbsp;
you land the big idiot and do some nasty stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.880
with it, or you fall flat. However, this design&nbsp;
of free-Polymorph-target-plus-value-engine in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.640
command zone, along with all the baked in utility,&nbsp;
could mean much better resilience. The deck also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.200
had a lot of land ramp sorceries, since that’s&nbsp;
what was required for its creature-and-artifact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.240
polymorph gameplan, and that sort of ramp base&nbsp;
along with Thrasios already makes for a difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.600
to stop engine. Finally, the three commander storm&nbsp;
cards all let this deck come surging back into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.480
game even after it’s been shut down, and the idea&nbsp;
of Echo Storming to create 5 copies of Nexus of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:09.120
Becoming is extremely tasty. So, what was this&nbsp;
deck’s problem? Why didn’t I end up building it?

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.440
The biggest issue was that the deck was simply too&nbsp;
generic, and generically mean at that. Thrasios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.640
plus ramp is a very generic gameplan. All of my&nbsp;
top end creatures were generic disgusting bombs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.800
with four being played in at least 7% of&nbsp;
in-color decks, and another three being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.080
infamously oppressive Praetors. The gameplan&nbsp;
had essentially zero synergistic core to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.400
because a polymorph deck with that many targets&nbsp;
can’t really have a synergistic core to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.280
the cards it finds can’t be anything but&nbsp;
generic soul-crushing bombs. Polymorphing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.120
a cool mechanism to arrive at those bombs,&nbsp;
but that doesn’t make them interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:49.280
either for me or for my opponents. So, the deck&nbsp;
got scrapped, never to be returned to. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.920
with each of these three decks I think it’s worth&nbsp;
asking—was there any of what excited me about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:59.200
original idea that could turn into an interesting&nbsp;
deck? Well, Rograkh polymorph does seem like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.760
workable core as an easy way to grab from a&nbsp;
selection of creatures while still having access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.360
to a second commander, but I’d want it to be&nbsp;
something more specific than Thrasios. If I were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.840
to take another crack at this idea it’d need to be&nbsp;
with a partner like Kydele or Sakashima that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.720
a bit more specificity, and then the things to be&nbsp;
polymorphed for would need to be far less generic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.680
The tricky thing here would be finding enough&nbsp;
different options that the deck would always be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.320
happy with—with fewer than three polymorph targets&nbsp;
the deck would be in total gimmick territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.360
while with too many the deck would probably end&nbsp;
up with a generic and boring gameplan once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.880
or alternatively a wildly inconsistent one.&nbsp;
Finding several different high-cost cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.520
that generally fill the same specific&nbsp;
niche within the deck is a tricky task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.640
but that’s the only way I’d want to touch&nbsp;
polymorph again as a primary feature of a deck.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.760
If you’re enjoying this video you should&nbsp;
hit all the buttons you see on your screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.320
each and every one of them. You can also sign up&nbsp;
for my Patreon or a YouTube membership if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.320
to support what I do here, and you’ll also get&nbsp;
access to a companion piece to this video where I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:06.000
talk about three more decks I haven’t given up on.&nbsp;
A preview of that will be at the end of the video.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
The next deck I didn’t finish is&nbsp;
one that I nicknamed Goblin Throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.560
and this one was far more recent. This is a&nbsp;
deck built around Throne of the Grim Captain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.400
a two mana artifact whose front side taps to mill&nbsp;
two cards and which can exile a Dinosaur, Merfolk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.840
Pirate, and Vampire from your board or graveyard&nbsp;
to flip. The backside is a 7/7 creature with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.160
a bunch of keywords that also has an on-attack&nbsp;
trigger, forcing opponents to sacrifice a nonland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.280
permanent and putting one of the typed-creatures&nbsp;
exiled with it into play tapped and attacking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.200
This is a fairly attractive hidden commander&nbsp;
to me for a bunch of reasons: the colors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.440
relatively flexible, the creature types it&nbsp;
uses feature a variety of interesting designs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.520
the card’s attributes make it findable with&nbsp;
a number of different conditional tutors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.240
and the front side of it is just a decently-costed&nbsp;
graveyard enabler in its own right. When I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.480
first deciding what I should do with this card,&nbsp;
I decided that Grixis was the easiest color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.280
identity to work with, since Blue was the primary&nbsp;
color for Merfolk, and red was a great secondary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.000
color for the other three types that also gave&nbsp;
better access haste. Then, I decided I wanted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.800
commander that was graveyard-based in some form&nbsp;
or other, sharing either a graveyard setup or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.240
graveyard payoff with Throne. I’m also a sucker&nbsp;
for a low-cost commander, so naturally I stumbled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.600
onto Norman Osborn, a looter guy on the two mana&nbsp;
side and a powerful discard payoff on the four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.800
mana side. This is a reasonably popular commander,&nbsp;
but the pairing with Throne of the Grim Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.680
is relatively less common. There’s a lot to work&nbsp;
with here, though: Surly Badgersaur and Flaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.800
Tyrannosaurus as madness-friendly dinos, all sorts&nbsp;
of looting pirates, multiple graveyard-friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.680
merfolk, and basically the entire vampire&nbsp;
subtype. We’ve also got the Incarnations to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.760
our stuff keywords when they’re in the graveyard,&nbsp;
a couple beautifully fitting extra combat cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:50.800
to let us Grim Captain even harder, and various&nbsp;
other unique cards that slot nicely into some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.400
role or other. All sorts of cards I find cool and&nbsp;
exciting managed to find their way into this deck:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:04.080
Strefan, Kiora the Rising Tide, Captain Howler,&nbsp;
Forbid, and Necrogoyf. So, what went wrong?

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.240
On the surface, everything looks fine. Unlike the&nbsp;
last deck, this one decidedly does have synergies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.920
its creatures do feel more intentionally chosen,&nbsp;
and its gameplan does seem more unique. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.640
my impression of the deck after thinking about it&nbsp;
longer is that it is simply less than the sum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:26.400
its parts, and the sheer power of its commander is&nbsp;
part of the issue here. I successfully identified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.520
that a discard subtheme within these colors and&nbsp;
with these creature types was going to work well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.120
but my commander actually makes that too easy.&nbsp;
Whereas other discard-oriented decks I play use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.920
unique payoffs like Sharuum or Ox of Agonas that&nbsp;
want to be in a pretty specific type of deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.480
Green Goblin simply lets me cast anything I&nbsp;
discarded that turn with a two-mana discount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.040
turning my cool discard plan into a torrent&nbsp;
of generic Magic: the Gathering gameplay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.040
Now, this might make it seem like the&nbsp;
issue primarily lies with the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:00.560
but really that’s not quite true. Using a&nbsp;
powerful, somewhat more generic discard commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.240
like Norman Osborn to support a more specific&nbsp;
and textured gameplan could be a cool idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:13.120
but Throne of the Grim Captain just isn’t that&nbsp;
cool as a thing to center a deck around. The card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:18.560
itself is a graveyard enabler and a fun payoff&nbsp;
that can be a fairly significant singular threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.760
but it’s not really well-situated to anchor a&nbsp;
gameplan. Likewise, the sorts of deck construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:28.720
it encourages, with a split of the four different&nbsp;
creature types, is kind of fun to build, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.800
doesn’t actually create a coherent gameplan to be&nbsp;
amplified. It’s “the Volo problem”—a card creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.720
constraints that are fun to deckbuild around but&nbsp;
which don’t actually create interesting gameplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:44.080
on their own. While this deck’s non-throne,&nbsp;
non-Goblin cards have some graveyard and discard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:49.120
themes that kinda work with one another, it was a&nbsp;
relatively generic midrangy affair on the whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:54.800
without enough of an identity to feel like&nbsp;
a coherent deck. So, I scrapped the idea.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.280
To be clear, I’m not against building a Throne&nbsp;
of the Grim Captain deck in the future—the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.080
still slaps even if I didn’t like this usage&nbsp;
of it. However, I think it’d essentially need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.960
to be a mostly-formed deck where the card&nbsp;
just happened to make sense, a deck where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.720
Throne could add spice to a strong existing&nbsp;
identity rather than being expected to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.360
identity all on its own. I actually did a little&nbsp;
experiment while writing this video where I tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.280
to slap together a different Throne of the Grim&nbsp;
Captain deck that solves the issues I have with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:28.080
the card in this deck. I went Mardu colors instead&nbsp;
of Grixis, with Kroxa and Kunoros in the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.960
zone instead of Norman Osborn. It was sort of&nbsp;
a graveyard cheese reanimator type situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.160
with all manner of spicy high cost dinosaurs and&nbsp;
vampires alongside a bunch of graveyard enablers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.600
and enough of the other types to get by. This deck&nbsp;
had a distinct identity with its different types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.840
a punchy aggressive playstyle, and a bunch&nbsp;
of congruence between the command zone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.840
Throne of the Grim Captain. The two can work&nbsp;
alongside each other, but they’re also rough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.760
analogs which is nice for if Throne gets&nbsp;
removed or if I’m struggling to keep the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:01.440
graveyard stocked for K&amp;K. So, every issue I&nbsp;
have with Goblin Throne is basically solved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:06.400
with this cool reanimator deck. It’s also not the&nbsp;
sort of deck I’m really interested in playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.960
kinda fragile and not wildly flexible in&nbsp;
its play paths. Just not my cup of tea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:14.720
This is the challenge of deckbuilding: there&nbsp;
are multiple elements that need to line up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.800
in order to make a deck I’m building worth&nbsp;
finishing, and sometimes solving one set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:24.080
issues I have with a deck just leads to an&nbsp;
entirely different set of issues. Learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:29.200
to identify those issues early in the process and&nbsp;
hold back on finishing a deck is a helpful thing.

