00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:52.000
The final deck I didn’t build is one I refer to&nbsp;
as “Enigmatic Control”, and this is the one I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.800
spent by far the most time thinking about.&nbsp;
The pivotal card of this deck was Enigmatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.280
Incarnation, a four mana enchantment in Simic&nbsp;
colors that allows its controller to sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:05.040
an enchantment on their end step, searching up&nbsp;
a creature with mana value one greater to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:10.400
into play. Immediately, this is appealing as an&nbsp;
unconventional birthing pod effect. Enchantment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.080
creatures are one obvious application here,&nbsp;
since an enchantment creature can be sacrificed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.720
to find a different enchantment creature just&nbsp;
like a normal pod effect, but there are other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:24.320
interesting options as well. Enchantments with&nbsp;
ETBs make for nice sacrifice targets, as do Sagas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.480
which have quite a few different options. There&nbsp;
are also a number of non-enchantment creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.600
to be grabbed that have great synergy with an&nbsp;
enchantment sacrificing gameplan, such as Tameshi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:39.280
Harnessed Snubhorn, and Moon-blessed Cleric. This&nbsp;
deck was looking to assemble a solid toolbox-y&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:44.080
sort of game plan with some of these different&nbsp;
options, although there was a final challenge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:49.600
when Enigmatic Incarnation triggered: on the end&nbsp;
step. This is a little bit of a problem for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.440
because it means we can’t get immediate use out&nbsp;
of any creatures we grab in the way we could with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.320
a conventional pod effect. No grabbing a card&nbsp;
draw creature to hit a land drop, no grabbing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.720
removal creature to enable a good attack step, no&nbsp;
grabbing a synergy piece creature to set up some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:08.720
sweet spells, and so forth. To me, this meant two&nbsp;
things: for one, flash enablers were going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:13.680
a notable part of our gameplan so that we can&nbsp;
play more on our opponents turns, and for two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.120
this deck’s general plan needed to be a bit slower&nbsp;
and more controlling. These two features combined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:24.480
with an enchantment-centered gameplan also made&nbsp;
a particular type of card very useful: rule of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:29.040
law effects. Preventing each player from casting&nbsp;
more than one spell each turn can do wonders for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.920
keeping people in check, especially in a deck that&nbsp;
has counterspells to stop even that one spell if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.560
it’s a bit too spooky. Realistically this means&nbsp;
that we need white in our color identity, so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:43.760
was at this point where I picked out a relatively&nbsp;
generic enchantment commander: Tuvasa the Sunlit.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:48.560
Tuvasa’s draw is nice for taking a bit of strain&nbsp;
off of our value engines, and the fact that Tuvasa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:54.160
herself gets decently big with enough enchantments&nbsp;
on board also allows her to be a wincon for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:58.160
This feature of the card is compounded by a&nbsp;
few evasion enchantments like Unquestioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:02.720
Authority and Thassa God of the Sea, and the&nbsp;
fact that these can simply be sacrificed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:06.400
find something else if the commander beatdown&nbsp;
plan doesn’t seem doable is a nice bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:11.440
flexibility. Aside from commander beatdown, this&nbsp;
deck had a handful of other options for winning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:17.600
most of them a little grittier. The deck featured&nbsp;
Karametra as a god that can give a ton of ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.920
and then there are some different options for&nbsp;
turning this mana advantage into a lock of sorts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:25.600
The deck had several different enchantress&nbsp;
effects, and these could be paired with Words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:31.360
of Wind to repeatedly bounce a ramp enchantment&nbsp;
stripping away resources from our opponents. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.840
deck also had Fear of Sleep Paralysis in it, which&nbsp;
could lock down opposing creatures if we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:39.840
playing enough enchantments. Both of these are&nbsp;
tricky to pull off with a rule of law effect still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.960
in play unless we have Heliod the Warped Eclipse&nbsp;
in play, but luckily for us our deck is built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:49.120
around sacrificing enchantments, so we have the&nbsp;
ability to get the rule of law enchantments out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:53.520
of there whenever we’re ready. Once we’re heavily&nbsp;
taxing our opponents ability to play the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:58.080
the idea was to beat them down with an army&nbsp;
of enchantment creatures and/or our commander.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.600
So, why didn’t I finish up and buy this deck?&nbsp;
Well, there were some pretty fundamental issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:09.440
mostly all stemming from the fact that Enigmatic&nbsp;
Incarnation is not a very good card. It’s slow as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:14.240
a primary engine since it’s 4 mana and triggers&nbsp;
on end step, and though it synergizes well with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:18.960
the Duskmourn Overlords, there are only&nbsp;
three of those available in the colors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:22.720
Maybe there could be a version of this deck&nbsp;
where we dip into the 7 other enchantments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.240
the game that can get heavily discounted&nbsp;
by certain requirements being fulfilled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:32.640
but these cards tend to be highly varied and not&nbsp;
necessarily conducive to a focused gameplan. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:37.600
trying to pump up the strength of a cool but&nbsp;
mediocre card is not necessarily a bad time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:41.120
and that is precisely what this deck&nbsp;
was trying to do. To accomplish this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:45.200
there are two main methods available to&nbsp;
enchantment decks: generic enchantress effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:50.960
and stax pieces. Both of these are fine, but they&nbsp;
do kind of clash with each other, particularly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.440
rule of law effects. Preventing players from&nbsp;
casting more than one spell per turn prevents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:00.000
playing more than one enchantment per turn to&nbsp;
draw cards, unless some of those enchantments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.800
have flash. We can try to make them have flash,&nbsp;
but there will still be a natural barrier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:11.040
overcome every game. I could dial in harder on&nbsp;
a strictly-enchantress gameplan, but that’d lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:16.000
a lot of the appeal of Enigmatic Incarnation—it&nbsp;
makes a lot of sense as a toolbox control card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:20.480
and if I’m building a generic enchantress deck&nbsp;
instead I don’t really feel like I’m building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:25.440
around it. As a final option we could mostly give&nbsp;
up on the generic enchantment synergies stuff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:30.800
focus on pushing our funky stax gameplan to&nbsp;
its limits, but I think cards like Sythis and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.840
Sanctum Weaver are kind of required to keep this&nbsp;
a mid-power deck, and the lower this deck’s power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:41.280
level dips, the worse its identity starts to&nbsp;
feel as a staxy toolbox control deck. Rule of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:45.840
law effects are pretty powerful stax effects, and&nbsp;
it doesn’t make sense to fight low-power gameplans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:50.480
with high-power stax. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;
bracket 2 style games should have no interaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:55.200
and in fact both of my bracket 2 decks have above&nbsp;
average counts of interaction cards, but the tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:00.480
being used should be a little lighter duty. So,&nbsp;
at the end of the day, I could come up with cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:05.200
play patterns around a cool card, interesting&nbsp;
branching gameplans for the deck to run down, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.840
those interesting gameplans didn’t flow together&nbsp;
in a way elegant enough to keep up in the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:14.880
commander game I wanted to keep up in. I could&nbsp;
do a lot of things, and end up in some powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.600
situations, but at its core Enigmatic Incarnation&nbsp;
just wasn’t a card that could give the deck the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:24.080
amount of smoothing it would need to feel good in&nbsp;
the average game, and that’s totally ignoring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:29.280
concept of an opponent melting it with a removal&nbsp;
spell immediately. So, I scrapped the deck.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.280
With all three of these decks, there was something&nbsp;
about them that excited me enough to spend a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:37.600
of time building them. I had some idea of the&nbsp;
possible challenges that the deck might face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:41.840
but it took me a little while to fully weigh&nbsp;
what I found cool about a deck versus what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:46.480
about it didn’t seem all that workable. With&nbsp;
that in mind, there are a couple exercises I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:52.240
like to go through now when I’m building decks to&nbsp;
avoid dedicating too much time to a faulty idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:56.320
The first of these is to think about the basic&nbsp;
plan for a deck, and then to consider how likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:01.520
it is that that plan will actually come together,&nbsp;
versus how likely it is that the deck will do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:05.840
something else. Ideally it’s pretty likely that&nbsp;
you’ll fulfill the specific vision you had in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:10.720
mind, but if it’s something more niche like a&nbsp;
weird combo or a hidden commander it might be nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.440
to make sure that the deck is fun even outside of&nbsp;
that. The Enigmatic Incarnation deck is an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:20.160
of one that kind of works with the enchantment&nbsp;
in play, but really does not function smoothly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:25.520
at all without it. By contrast, my Sharuum&nbsp;
Astral Slide deck needs the slide to hit its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:30.720
full lategame potential, but it’s a reasonably&nbsp;
fun artifact deck even before that happens.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:35.360
The other thing to consider is how your deck’s&nbsp;
game plan fits into the context of an actual game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:39.440
with actual opponents. What sorts of decks will&nbsp;
your deck be playing against based on its power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:44.480
level? What will their experience with your deck&nbsp;
look like? Will your deck be good for multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:50.080
enjoyable games, or will its gameplan get stale&nbsp;
for you and/or your opponents fairly rapidly? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:55.600
will usually be the bane of Polymorph and other&nbsp;
cheesy strategies for me—they’ll clear the bar of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:59.760
being cool in theory, but in practice they’re&nbsp;
often boring to play against more than once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:03.920
and without that freshness I simply don’t find&nbsp;
them interesting to play on my side of the table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:08.320
either. This isn’t to say that I can never make&nbsp;
a cheese deck work—maybe a bunch of other factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:13.200
all line up to make a deck enjoyable for me—and&nbsp;
it’s also not to say that what I find fun is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:17.520
you should find fun. You might very well be able&nbsp;
to take one of the decks I’ve mentioned today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:22.080
tune it to your preferences, and then have&nbsp;
a blast with it, and that’s great. What’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:26.240
important is that you know what you enjoy and what&nbsp;
you don’t enjoy, and that you’re able to check in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:30.560
with yourself about a deck before dedicating too&nbsp;
much time to it. Remove the blinders of a cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:36.320
idea and give your deck a good proper look, and&nbsp;
if it’s still looking just as shiny, go for it!

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:40.400
Thanks for watching, and thanks to Archidekt&nbsp;
for supporting the channel. A big part of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.480
makes it possible for me to start building so&nbsp;
many decks and not even delete the majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:49.440
them is Archidekt’s folder system. The fact that I&nbsp;
can separate my current decks from my prospective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:54.160
decks from all the other random things I do like&nbsp;
precon reviews lets me stay organized, even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:58.400
dozens and dozens of decks on my profile. A&nbsp;
link to all my decks is in the description if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:02.480
you want to check those out, including the ones&nbsp;
I’ve mentioned today. You can also check out my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.480
Tymna Kediss list I’ve been thinking about, a deck&nbsp;
that I’ve put on the backburner but haven’t yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:12.000
abandoned yet. I have a few decks like that, and&nbsp;
I talk about them in a bonus second part of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:17.280
video only available to patrons and YouTube&nbsp;
members. A preview of that begins right now!

